Falsifies `machine_discovery::acpi::verification::a_table_header_this_parser_accepts_always_has_a_body_length`.

The defect: the length guard asks whether the table claims a length at all instead
of whether it claims one long enough to cover its own header. `SdtHeader::body_len`
is `length - SDT_HEADER_LEN` with no guard of its own, so a table whose `length`
field is between 1 and 35 underflows a `usize` to roughly 2^64, and every caller
that reads `body_len()` bytes after the header walks off the end of the ACPI region.

This is the shape a reader reaches for while tidying: "a length of zero is
obviously wrong" is true and is not the condition the subtraction needs. The
file's own test supplies `length = 10`, which this defect still catches, so the
test stays green while the property is false for `length = 1` through `9` and
`11` through `35`.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(h.length as usize, SDT_HEADER_LEN + h.body_len())`,
or the arithmetic-overflow check inside `body_len` itself.

